class Solution {
public:
    bool searchMatrix(vector<vector<int>>& matrix, int target) {

        for(int i=0;i<matrix.size();i++){
          //  if(matrix[i]==target)return true;
            for(int j=0;j<matrix[i].size();j++){
                if(matrix[i][j]==target){
                    return true;
                }
            }
        }
        return false;
    }
};

explanation:
for(int j = 0; j < matrix[i].size(); j++)
Description (Simple)
j = 0 → Start from the first column of the current row.
j < matrix[i].size() → Continue until all columns in the current row have been visited.
j++ → Move to the next column after checking the current one.
In one sentence

This loop traverses every column of the current row (i) in the matrix.

Example
matrix =
{
    {1, 3, 5},
    {7, 9, 11},
    {13, 15, 17}
};

If i = 1, then the current row is:

{7, 9, 11}

The loop runs like this:

j = 0 → matrix[1][0] = 7
j = 1 → matrix[1][1] = 9
j = 2 → matrix[1][2] = 11

After j = 2, j becomes 3.

Now the condition:

j < matrix[1].size()   // 3 < 3

is false, so the inner loop stops and the outer loop moves to the next row.

Remember:

i represents the row index.
j represents the column index.
